+++
title = "Configuration"
template = "docs"
+++

Revela stores project settings in `project.json` in your project folder. The project wizard creates it when you start `revela` in a folder that has no project yet (see [Installation](../../get-started/installation/#first-run)). `revela config project` creates it too.

## Quick Configuration

Use the interactive config menu:

```bash
revela config
```

Or configure specific settings directly:

```bash
revela config theme --set Lumina
revela config paths --source "D:\Photos" --output "dist"
revela config image --formats avif:80,webp:85,jpg:90
```

`revela config site` edits `site.json`. It is interactive only and asks for each field the theme defines.

## Configuration File Structure

```json
{
  "project": {
    "name": "my-portfolio",
    "baseUrl": "https://example.com",
    "basePath": "/"
  },
  "paths": {
    "source": "source",
    "output": "output"
  },
  "theme": {
    "name": "Lumina"
  },
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      "webp": 85,
      "jpg": 90,
      "avif": 80
    },
    "sorting": {
      "galleries": "asc",
      "images": { "field": "dateTaken", "direction": "desc" }
    }
  }
}
```

> **Note:** Image sizes are defined by the theme in its `Configuration/images.json`, not in `project.json` (see [Themes](../themes/#theme-structure)). Every photo gets the theme sizes smaller than the photo, plus its original width. Image format quality (`webp`, `jpg`, `avif`) are flat properties under `generate.images`. Set one to `0` to disable that format.

> **Language belongs in `site.json`.** A `language` key in the `project` section of `project.json` is rejected with a configuration error. Set `"language": "en"` in `site.json` instead (see [Site Metadata](#site-metadata)).

## Dependencies and Feeds

> **Full edition and .NET tool only.** The Standalone edition has every official theme and plugin built in and has no commands that install packages, so it doesn't need this section.

Themes and plugins your project needs are listed under `dependencies`. The global `revela.json` and your `project.json` use the same shape; for the same key, `project.json` wins.

```jsonc
{
  "theme": { "name": "Lumina" },
  "dependencies": {
    "feeds": {
      "test": "../my-feed",                          // folder, relative to this file
      "myFeed": "https://example.com/v3/index.json"   // remote NuGet v3 feed
    },
    "packages": {
      "Spectara.Revela.Themes.Lumina": "1.0.0",
      "Acme.Revela.Watermark": "1.0.0"
    }
  }
}
```

- **`dependencies.packages`** maps a package ID to an exact version. `revela plugin install`, `revela theme install` and `revela restore` write the version that was actually installed. A missing version or `"latest"` means "newest stable version" (a prerelease build of Revela also picks prereleases).
- **Any package ID works.** Revela reads whether a package is a theme or a plugin from the package itself, so third-party packages are restored like official ones.
- **`dependencies.feeds`** maps a name to a NuGet v3 URL or a folder of `.nupkg` files. Relative folders are resolved from the file that declares them. nuget.org and the bundled `packages/` folder are always available.
- **`theme.name`** is the theme's name from its manifest (for example `Lumina`), not a package ID. `revela restore` looks it up in your local and installed themes and falls back to the official `Spectara.Revela.Themes.<name>` package only when nothing else provides that name.

> **Feeds from a project need your confirmation.** A feed that appears only in `project.json` (not in your global `revela.json`) could come from someone else's repository. `revela restore`, `revela plugin install` and `revela theme install` list such feeds and ask before using them. In scripts and CI, pass `--allow-project-feeds` if you trust them, or add the feed to your global config with `revela config feed add`.

## JSONC: Comments and Trailing Commas

Every Revela configuration file accepts a slightly relaxed JSON dialect, commonly called **JSONC**. You can use:

- **Line comments**, like `// this`
- **Block comments**, like `/* this */`
- **Trailing commas** at the end of objects and arrays

```jsonc
{
  "theme": {
    "name": "Lumina"
  },
  "generate": {
    "images": {
      // Quality per format (0 disables that format)
      "webp": 85,
      "jpg": 90,
      "avif": 80,
    },
  },
}
```

This applies to `project.json`, `site.json`, `theme.json`, `images.json`, and the global `revela.json` (see [Where Settings Come From](#where-settings-come-from)).

> **Heads up:** When Revela rewrites one of these files for you, for example with `revela plugin install <id>` or any `revela config` command, comments and your original formatting are **not preserved**. The file is reserialised as pretty-printed JSON. This matches how tools like `npm` and `dotnet user-secrets` behave. If you keep important comments in a file, prefer editing it manually.

### Editor support

VS Code in the Revela source tree treats every `*.json` file as JSONC. If you author your own project in another editor and see lint warnings on `//` comments, associate `project.json` / `site.json` with the JSONC language.

## Custom Paths

By default, Revela uses a `source/` folder for your images and `output/` for the generated site. You can customize these paths for advanced workflows.

### Configure via CLI

```bash
# Interactive
revela config paths

# Direct
revela config paths --source "D:\OneDrive\Photos\Portfolio" --output "dist"
```

### Use Cases

<div class="features-grid">

### ☁️ Cloud Storage Source

Point source to a OneDrive, Dropbox, or iCloud folder. Your photos sync automatically, Revela generates from them.

### 🌐 Direct Deployment

Output directly to your webserver directory (`/var/www/html`) without copying files.

### 📁 Shared Photo Library

Multiple Revela projects can share the same source folder with different filters.

</div>

### Example: OneDrive Integration

```json
{
  "paths": {
    "source": "D:\\OneDrive\\Photos\\Portfolio",
    "output": "output"
  }
}
```

Your workflow:
1. Add photos to OneDrive on any device
2. They sync to your computer
3. Run `revela generate all` to rebuild

### Example: Direct Server Deployment

```json
{
  "paths": {
    "source": "source",
    "output": "/var/www/html/photos"
  }
}
```

After `revela generate all`, your site is immediately live, with no file copying needed.

### Path Resolution

| Path Type | Example | Resolves To |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Relative | `source` | `{project}/source` |
| Relative | `../photos` | `{parent}/photos` |
| Absolute | `D:\Photos` | `D:\Photos` |
| Absolute | `/var/www/html` | `/var/www/html` |

> **Output is disposable.** `revela clean output` deletes the whole output directory, and `revela clean images` deletes unused folders inside it. Both commands refuse to run, and delete nothing, if `output` is a drive or filesystem root, your home directory, the project folder, the source folder, or a folder that contains any of them. Keep `output` in a folder that only Revela writes to.

## Available Config Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `revela config` | Interactive configuration menu |
| `revela config project` | Project name and URL (`--name`, `--url`) |
| `revela config theme` | Theme and photo viewer (`--set`, `--viewer`, `--clear-viewer`) |
| `revela config site` | Site metadata in `site.json` (interactive) |
| `revela config paths` | Source and output directories (`--source`, `--output`) |
| `revela config image` | Image formats and quality (`--formats`) |
| `revela config sorting` | Gallery and image sort order |
| `revela config onedrive` / `serve` / `statistics` | Plugin settings |
| `revela config feed` | NuGet package sources (Full edition and .NET tool only) |
| `revela config locations` | Show configuration file paths |

Run `revela check config` to validate your configuration files without building. See the [CLI Reference](../../reference/cli-reference/#config) for every option.

## Site Metadata

Site-specific content (title, description, copyright, language) is stored separately in `site.json`:

```json
{
  "title": "My Photography Portfolio",
  "author": "Jane Smith",
  "description": "Landscapes and nature photography",
  "copyright": "© 2026 Jane Smith",
  "language": "en"
}
```

`language` defaults to `en`. It sets the page language, selects the theme's UI text (see [Theme Language](../themes/#theme-language)), and controls month and day names, for example in calendars and `format_date`.

Configure with:

```bash
revela config site
```

This separation allows you to:
- Share `project.json` settings across projects
- Keep personal branding in `site.json`
- Use different site metadata for staging/production

## Page-Scoped Assets

Project-specific CSS and JavaScript files live under `themes/{theme-name}/Assets/` and are
declared in `site.json`. Use a string for an asset needed everywhere, or an object to limit it
to page, template, feature, or plugin scopes:

```json
{
  "stylesheets": [
    "website.css",
    { "path": "prism.css", "scope": ["docs"] }
  ],
  "scripts": [
    { "path": "prism.js", "scope": ["docs"] }
  ]
}
```

Common scopes are `index`, `gallery`, `photo`, exact template names such as `docs`, and
plugin prefixes such as `statistics` or `calendar`. The `lightbox` feature scope is applied
only when the current page resolves to the Lightbox photo viewer.

Assets without a declaration are still copied to `_assets`, but are not added to generated
HTML. See [revela.website](../themes/website-example/) for the complete customization example.

## Where Settings Come From

Revela merges configuration from several sources. Later sources override earlier ones:

1. Built-in defaults
2. Global `revela.json`: next to the Revela executable when that folder is writable (for example an extracted Standalone ZIP), otherwise `%APPDATA%/Revela/` on Windows or `~/.config/Revela/` on Linux/macOS. `revela config locations` shows the path in use.
3. Project `project.json`
4. Environment variables prefixed `SPECTARA__REVELA__` (use `__` to separate nested keys)
5. Command-line arguments

This lets you override a single setting for one run without editing a file. For example, `SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__SERVE__PORT=3000` changes the dev-server port temporarily.

### Plugin Settings

Every plugin keeps its settings below `plugins`, under a short key the plugin defines:

| Plugin | Key | Environment variable prefix |
|--------|-----|-----------------------------|
| Serve | `serve` | `SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__SERVE__` |
| Statistics | `statistics` | `SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__STATISTICS__` |
| Source.OneDrive | `oneDrive` | `SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__ONEDRIVE__` |
| Source.Calendar | `calendarFeeds` | `SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__CALENDARFEEDS__` |

```json
{
  "plugins": {
    "serve": { "port": 3000 },
    "statistics": { "maxEntriesPerCategory": 20 }
  }
}
```

If a key below `plugins` is not used by any installed plugin (for example the typo `serv`), Revela prints a warning and suggests the closest key.
